so should we just give up gareth do we  just go home because family-wise error  rate is just impossible or is there an  easy solution that we can just apply  straight out of the box so so there are  there are a number of classical methods  that we can use to control a family-wise  zero rate these methods tend to work  better for let's say medium values for m  and danielle is going to go through some  more modern approaches for really large  values of m but let's let's start by  talking about a couple of the classical  methods that you can at least use of m  doesn't get too large  and so the first one and by far the most  popular i think is this approach called  the bonferroni correction  and it's based on this relatively simple  probabilistic calculation which says  that the family-wise error rate is equal  to  the probability of the union of these  events aj where aj is just the event  that we make a mis type one mistake we  falsely reject the jth null hypothesis  and if you've done any kind of  probability class you'll know that the  probability of the union of a whole lot  of events  is bounded above it can't be larger than  the sum of each of the individual  probabilities  so that puts sort of an upper bound on  the family-wise error rate here  and it suggests that if we take each one  of these hypothesis tests and we only  reject if the p-value is less than alpha  over m  so that means that the family-wise error  rate can't be more than the sum of m  different alpha over m's or in other  words m times alpha over m or just alpha  then we can control the family y zero  rate at level alpha  by only rejecting when the p value for  an individual test is less than alpha  over m  so what does this mean we can break this  down and make this a little bit simpler  if you want your family-wise error rate  to be no bigger than 0.05 but you've  tested 100 null hypotheses then you  should reject any null hypothesis with a  p-value smaller than .05 divided by a  hundred  and if you want to control family-wise  error rate at level 0.01 then it should  be 0.01 divided by 100. exactly  so  it's this is a popular approach i think  for two reasons it's guaranteed to  control the family y08 and it's a very  simple approach to implement just  calculate your p-values the standard way  that you would for any hypothesis test  and then you just apply a more stringent  threshold with a simple calculation  so just to illustrate this idea on some  sample data here this is a data set out  of the book this is fund manager data  where  we've looked at five different fund  managers  gareth works at a business school just  in case you're watching i i i i did  mention that i work at it oh gareth by  the way he's a dean so we shouldn't  tease him too much  i haven't noticed you holding back so  far but i'm gonna i'm gonna start  remembering now yeah yeah yeah i'm not  your dean so it doesn't matter yeah  that's true i'm totally off the hook so  here we have the excess return so this  just measures how much these funds  outperform the stock market in general  over this period of time that we looked  at so the three next to the fund manager  for for manager one just represents that  on average that fund was got a three  percent higher return annually than the  stock market in general s here just  represents the standard deviation of  those returns and the p values here are  just correspond to the null hypothesis  that the fund manager is doing no better  than the stock market these returns are  just  noisy versions of the stock market if  you like so then i'll how about just to  be clear the null hypothesis here for  each of the five managers is that the  true mean return is zero  true excess mean return true actually  you can see i don't work in a business  school the true excess mean return is  zero  the observed excess mean return for each  of the managers is like three negative  point one and so on exactly so the  question is is is three or negative  point one far enough away from zero that  we're convinced that the manager is  actually doing something other than just  randomly picking stocks  and so you can see some of these  p-values are pretty small and and some  of them are pretty large in particular  manager one and manager three have  pretty small p-values so if we were just  to look at one hypothesis test at a time  then we would probably say okay the  first and the third managers have  statistically significant non-zero  returns they seem to be doing better  than the stock market as a whole  but here we've failed to take account of  the fact that we're actually doing five  separate hypothesis tests and m is is  five in this case we're doing multiple  tests so we should really be doing  something like a bonferroni correction  to adjust for that  and  so that means that the family-wise error  rate here is almost certainly greater  than five percent if we're just looking  at these individually so if we do a bond  for any correction here and we want a  family y zero rate of five percent then  the bonferroni correction says all we  need to do is take .05 and divide by m  which is five here and use one percent  as our cutoff instead of five percent  and when we do that  then  unfortunately now only the first manager  that's the only one we can be certain is  outperforming the stock market because  you see that manager three here now has  a p value that's above one percent our  new bonferroni cutoff and so we can't be  sure after adjusting for multiple  testing that manager three is actually  outperforming the stock market so very  simple approach but the  negative side effect here is we can't  reject as many hypotheses so there is  something weird about multiple testing  which is sort of like  um  sometimes hard to wrap your head around  which is it's just all about honesty  because if you just looked at this data  and you're like oh like i see that um  manager one has like the largest two  statistic and the smallest p value so  i'm just going to like toss the other  ones and you just pretend that there  weren't actually five managers then you  wouldn't need to do any multiple testing  correction the only reason you need to  correct for multiple testing is because  you know in your heart that you're a  good and honest person and part of being  a good and honest person is that you  know that there were five managers and  you have to account for that in your  hypothesis testing but it can often feel  very very tempting for people not to  fully reckon with the fact that there  were more than there was more than one  manager to begin with in fact there were  five  and even if people aren't trying to be  deliberately deceptive they can often  just misunderstand and not know that  they need to be adjusting for this fact  right like you look at your data a  little bit you're like oh this one looks  interesting let me test this one but but  without accounting for the fact that  they  in a way consider testing a lot of null  hypotheses and that can lead to a  multiple testing problem
 

